  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 237 PM EDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\nCurrent-Tonight...Seasonably warm and humid conditions this \nafternoon. Gradually strengthening low pressure off of the Mid Atlc \ncoast will drag a weak \"cool\" front south into north-central FL this \nevening. Deep moisture along/ahead of this feature has promoted \nearly initiation of SCT-NMRS showers/lightning storms across ECFL. \nExpect activity to continue to focus south/inland thru the afternoon \nand early evening. ISOLD strong storms are possible. Other storm \nimpacts remain locally gusty winds of 40-50 mph, frequent lightning \nstrikes, and torrential downpours of 1-3\" locally in a few strong \nstorms or from repeated rounds - potentially leading to minor \nflooding. Storm steering remains rather weak. While convection is \nforecast to diminish into evening, ISOLD-SCT onshore-moving showers \nand storms will remain possible overnight affecting mainly coastal\ncounties. Overnight lows will be in the L-M70s. \n  \nThu-Fri...As upper ridging amplifies from southeast TX thru the \nPlains/Upper Midwest, a mid/upper level trough across the \nAppalachians will progress southward, exiting the Carolinas and \nemerging across the western Atlc Thu evening/early Fri and \ncontinuing to slide slowly eastward thru late in the period. At the \nsurface, low pressure off of the mid Atlc coast will continue to \nstrengthen as it races up the Eastern Seaboard. The aforementioned\nsurface front will continue to push across ECFL and into the \nsouthern FL peninsula. A rather tight pressure gradient develops \nwith breezy to windy N/NE winds developing across the area on Thu,\nremaining elevated Thu night, and continuing into Fri. This will \nlead to hazardous boating and beach conditions. Sustained wind \nspeeds of 15-25 mph with gusts up to 30-35 mph are forecast north\nof Okeechobee County and the Treasure Coast on Thu, with highest \nwind speeds/gusts focused at the coast. Sustained winds 15-20 mph,\nwith gusts 25-30 mph then spread southward on Fri. SCT-NMRS \nshowers and ISOLD-SCT storms forecast again on Thu (PoPs 30-70%) \nwith passing front. Drier air will then lower rain chances into \nFri, with only low end rain chances (20-30%) focused along the \ncoast. \n\nHighs in the M-U80s on Fri, perhaps L80s for much of Volusia County, \ndrop slightly Fri into the L-M80s areawide. Overnight lows will \nremain in the L-M70s Thu night, then as drier/cooler air moves into \nthe area - will see mins in the M-U60s for much of the region Fri \nnight-Sat morning. Some coastal locations near to south of the Cape, \nhowever, may remain in the L-M70s.  \n\nSat-Tue...Previous Modified...Drier and slightly cooler conditions \nforecast into the weekend through early next week, giving us our \nfirst taste of Fall. While highs still reach into the 80s, overnight \nlows drop into the 60s for the first time since early to mid June \n(see climate section for last dates below). Noticeably lower \nhumidity will also make conditions more comfortable through the \nperiod. Mostly dry conditions forecast this weekend, with a gradual \nincrease in rain chances Mon-Tue as moisture slowly builds. Breezy \nN/NE winds may continue along the coast into Sat, but winds subside \nthrough Sun and into early next week as the pressure gradient\nrelaxes.",
